  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\n07z/2am surface analysis shows a sprawling ridge of high\npressure extending from Quebec southwestward into the \nmid-Mississippi River Valley. Despite synoptic subsidence\nbeneath the ridge and dry northeasterly flow from the Great\nLakes, ample boundary layer moisture remains trapped beneath an\ninversion. While skies partially cleared yesterday afternoon,\nthe low overcast has returned everywhere along/east of a Peoria\nto Springfield line early this morning. While the westward\nadvance of the cloud shield has slowed somewhat over the past\nhour or two, think overcast conditions will exist across the\nentire KILX CWA by sunrise. Forecast soundings strongly suggest\nthe low-level moisture will remain locked in place today:\nhowever, the RRFS sky forecast once again offers hope for some\npartial sunshine this afternoon...particularly along/west of the\nI-55 corridor. Given cloud cover and a cool NE breeze, high\ntemperatures will remain below normal for this time of year in\nthe upper 60s to around 70 degrees. Any partial clearing that\noccurs will vanish by tonight as low clouds once again spread\nand/or develop westward across the area after dark.\n\nThe upper air pattern currently features a weak Rex Block with\na high over southern Canada and a low across central Illinois\ninto the Ohio River Valley. Models continue to show the block\ngradually shifting eastward and breaking down as the low deepens\nover the Mid-Atlantic region. With an increasingly strong upper\nlow to the east essentially blocking the progress of any systems\nacross the central/eastern CONUS, a narrow upper ridge will form\nover the Midwest and remain in place through early next week.\nDespite the presence of the ridge, cloud cover will be slow to\nclear from central Illinois as boundary layer flow remains E/NE\ninto the weekend. Am therefore expecting mostly cloudy skies \nagain on Thursday before partial sunshine finally returns by \nFriday.\n\nA short-wave trough crossing the Rockies into the Plains will\nrun into the blocking pattern and come to a halt over the\nweekend. This system will come close enough to cause an \nincrease in cloud cover and perhaps some light showers west of \nthe I-55 corridor Saturday/Sunday. All models eventually weaken \nthe wave as the narrow ridge axis holds firm. As a result, any \nrain chances across the western KILX CWA will come to an end by \nSunday afternoon/Monday as temperatures climb back to seasonal \nnormals in the middle to upper 70s. After that, the Mid-Atlantic\nupper low is progged to weaken and eject northeastward as a \nprominent trough swings across the Plains by early next week. As\nthis wave and its associated cold front approach, boundary \nlayer flow will become S/SW and temps will soar into the 80s by \nnext Tuesday. The next significant chance for precipitation \nwill arrive with the front, which is currently projected to push\ninto central Illinois Tuesday night into Wednesday.",
